  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows center of upper high pressure \nlocated across nrn TX/OK with ridge axis pointed northeast into \nPacific NW. Monsoonal moisture continues to stream around the \nperiphery of the ridge. Minor waves are located across eastern \nID/southwest MT/western WY with another lifting northward through \nUT. At the surface, high pressure located across the eastern \nDakotas with a weak lee trough located along the Big Horns. \nTemperatures at 18Z across the CWA range from the 60s in the cloud\ncovered areas across the east, to the mid 70s to lower 80s in the\npartly cloudy areas. \n\nThe biggest issue facing the near-term forecast will be the \nexpected rainfall overnight into Thursday. Models have backed off \non strength/timing and track of the wave now moving through MT/WY.\nDeterministic models now have this feature sliding just a bit \nfurther south than previous runs, taking expected rainfall also a \nbit further south. Expected pwats are still high, running 150-200%\nof normal overnight through Thursday. Ensembles show probability \nof 1.75\"+ pwats increasing to 60-90% during the day Thursday \nacross our southern counties. However, ensembles also show \nprobabilities of receiving 0.25\" or higher pcpn only on the order \nof 30-40%, and again mainly relegated to areas mainly south of \nI-90. Things remain pretty transitory so not too worried about any\nflooding/flash flooding issues. Still wouldn't be surprised to \nhear of some localized 1\"+ rainfall amounts by the close of day \nThursday, but thinking now it won't be as widespread as earlier \nthought. \n\nAfter that, hot and dry will be the rule heading into next week.\nWith a more spotty nature to the rainfall, there may not be as\nmuch moisture to burn off on Friday than previously thought.\nDeterministic models have actually trended higher for forecasted\nhighs on Friday. With 700hpa temps pushing +20C and 850hpa temps\n+36C by 00Z Sunday, not concerned about any convection developing\nover the Black Hills. Will most likely need heat headlines (again)\nfor the upcoming weekend.",
